.TH FDSTRESSTEST 1
.SH NAME
fdstresstest \- exercise concurrent fd use against a shared descriptor group
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fdstresstest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Fdstresstest
spawns sixteen processes that deliberately
.I share
the parent's file descriptor group \(em no
.B Sys->NEWFD
\(em and has each open, write, read and drop
.B /dev/null
eighty times, so many processes enter and leave blocking system calls
against one descriptor table at once.
.PP
A hang is the failure.
When it succeeds it prints a count and
.BR PASS ;
when it fails it produces no output and never exits, so run it under an
external timeout:
.EX
	timeout 30 emu-g -c0 -r . /dis/fdstresstest.dis
.EE
It used to hang, and that is what it was written for.
Measured on the build that had the bug: 67 hangs in 150 runs under
.BR -c1 ,
26 in 80 under
.BR -c0 .
It now passes.
.SH "THE BUG IT FOUND"
.IR Emu (1)'s
.B unlock
had no release barrier.
It called
.BR coherencefn ,
a function pointer set once in
.B main
to an empty function on every platform, and then stored zero into the
lock word with a plain store.
On x86 that is nearly harmless; on arm64 the stores a critical section
made could become visible after the store that released the lock, so the
next thread to take it \(em whose
.B _tas
does have acquire semantics \(em could read state the previous holder
had finished writing but not published.
A process pushed onto
.B isched.vmq
could therefore vanish from that queue with nobody popping it, which is
exactly the state described below.
.PP
One instruction:
.B "str wzr, [x19]"
became
.BR "stlr wzr, [x19]" .
Interleaved against the unfixed binary in one session, 150 pairs:
67 hangs to none.
See
.B doc/hpc-plan.md
for the full account, including why three earlier attempts missed it.
.SH "THE HANG, AS IT WAS"
Kept because it is what the test looks for, and because a regression
would look like this again.
Sampling the native threads of a hung
.IR emu (1)
shows the deadlock clearly.
One thread is blocked in
.B acquire
.RI ( emu/port/dis.c ),
reached from
.B Sys_open
\(em so it is waiting for a virtual machine slot, not for the file.
It sits on
.BR isched.vmq .
Every other virtual machine thread is parked in
.BR osblock ,
either inside
.B iyield
having just handed its slot to someone else, or asleep in
.B Sleep
on
.BR isched.irend .
Nothing is left to take the waiting process off
.B isched.vmq
and
.B osready
it.
.PP
The obvious suspects are not the cause.
.B Osblock
and
.B osready
are a counting semaphore taken under a mutex, so a wakeup delivered
before the sleep is not lost.
.B Tready,
the predicate the idle threads sleep on, does test
.B isched.vmq
and not only
.BR isched.runhd ,
so a process arriving on that queue is a reason to wake.
.B Incref
and
.B decref
are lock-protected, so this is not the
.B Chan
reference counting.
None of them was the cause, and neither was the accounting between
.BR acquire ,
.B release
and
.BR iyield ,
which was audited and found sound.
The queue really was manipulated only under
.BR isched.l ;
what failed was that releasing that lock published nothing.
.PP
Note that
.B acquire
only calls
.B Wakeup
when the process it just queued was the first on
.BR isched.vmq .
That is sound while some thread is asleep on
.BR isched.irend ,
and does nothing when they are all parked in
.B iyield
instead.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/fdstresstest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR emu (1)
.SH BUGS
It is a regression test for one specific fault and cannot show that the
scheduler is correct, only that this hang has not come back.
.PP
Only the release side of the lock was ever wrong, and only that was
changed.
.BR Lock ()'s
spin abandons the exclusive monitor on each failed attempt without
.BR clrex ,
which nothing here examines.
.PP
It has no internal watchdog on purpose.
Adding one stopped the hang reproducing entirely \(em the extra
.B spawn
and
.B alt
perturb scheduling enough to close the window \(em so a watchdog would
have quietly turned this into a test that always passes.
.PP
Because it hangs rather than failing, it is not suitable for an
unattended test run without a timeout.
